# The lexical extension

`lexical` ranks a corpus by the words it shares with a query. It is the
retrieval HQL always has: no index, no model file, no configuration, nothing to
build. Written out with `import lexical`, it supplies one step.

```hql
import lexical

cards
| lexical("bearer token authorization")
| take(5)
| table
```

## What it computes

Each document and the query become a hashed bag of words: every word longer
than one character is lowercased, hashed into one of 256 buckets by FNV-1a, and
weighted by damped term frequency — `1 + ln(count)` — so a long document does
not outrank a precise one merely by repeating a word. The vectors are
L2-normalised and compared by cosine. The hash is fixed, so the bucket a word
lands in never depends on the build.

A card scoring nothing is dropped rather than ranked last, because zero
similarity is not a weak answer but the absence of one. Ties break on the card's
own ordering key, so a prefix never depends on the order a vault loaded in.

The text it sees is the card's title, its authored header strings and its body —
the same text [[semantic]] embeds, and the contract is in
[HQL-0002](../../../proposals/HQL-0002/README.md).

## What it does not compute

It models no meaning. A query and a document that share no word score zero
however plainly one answers the other: against the birds corpus,
`lexical("night hunting birds")` finds no owl, because the owl articles say
"nocturnal" and "after dark" and never "night". That is not a defect to be fixed
inside `lexical` — it is what matching spellings is — and it is why [[semantic]]
exists beside it rather than on top of it.

The name is the point. It was called `semantic` once, and a `Hit` that told
every consumer a model had produced its score was making a claim
`hql.hashbag.v1` could not support. A score is evidence, and evidence whose
provenance misdescribes it is worse than no evidence.

## What a ranking reports

Every `Hit` carries the `Retrieval` that produced it: the query, the index (the
vault's root), `model: hql.hashbag.v1`, the crate version as `revision`,
`metric: cosine`, and `approximate: false` — the scoring is exact brute force
over every card it was handed.

See [[hql/semantic-search]] for the vocabulary, [[hql/extensions]] for the
registry, and [[orderable]] for why a ranking carries its own order.
